  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two lithium-ion solvation environments and how they affect electron transfer and lithium deposition at a metal surface. On the left, a Li+ ion coordinated by G2 and HFE is shown with weak orbital overlap, marked by slow electron transfer. In this case, electrons appear to reduce the solvent more readily, and the lithium metal surface grows into rough, uneven Li dendrites. The image suggests that poor electronic coupling between the solvated ion complex and the electrode promotes parasitic solvent reduction and nonuniform deposition.\n\nOn the right, replacing G2 with MTP creates a different coordination structure around Li+ together with HFE. This arrangement is labeled as having strong orbital overlap and fast electron transfer. The result is lower reactivity of the solvent and a much more uniform lithium deposit, illustrated as a smooth, rounded layer rather than dendritic protrusions.\n\nOverall, the figure’s main message is that tailoring the solvation shell can change interfacial electron-transfer kinetics. Stronger orbital interaction in the electrolyte helps direct electrons toward Li+ reduction instead of solvent decomposition, leading to safer, more even lithium plating.",
